      {
        "id": "p5-q2b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Complete the table of values and calculate $\\frac{q}{\\sqrt p}$ for each pair $(q,\\sqrt p)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The $\\sqrt p$ row is $0,1,2,3,4$. The ratios are undefined, $4,4,4,4$, respectively.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt0,\\sqrt1,\\sqrt4,\\sqrt9,\\sqrt{16})&=(0,1,2,3,4)\\\\\\frac41&=4\\\\\\frac82&=4\\\\\\frac{12}3&=4\\\\\\frac{16}4&=4\\end{align*}$$\nAt $(p,q)=(0,0)$, the ratio is $\\frac{0}{0}$, which is undefined. For each non-zero pair, $\\frac{q}{\\sqrt p}=4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q2c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Assuming that there is a simple relationship between the two variables, find a formula for $q$ in terms of $p$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$q=4\\sqrt p$",
          "worked": "The non-zero pairs have constant ratio $\\frac{q}{\\sqrt p}=4$, so $q$ is directly proportional to $\\sqrt p$.\n$$\\begin{align*}q&=k\\sqrt p\\\\4&=k\\sqrt1\\\\k&=4\\\\q&=4\\sqrt p\\end{align*}$$\nThis formula also gives $q=0$ when $p=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
